WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.600
To learn what a part is for, take it away.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.760
But which part should you take away?

3
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.610
The one idea gives three choices.

4
00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:12.670
Remove the pigment.

5
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:14.730
Remove the rods and cones.

6
00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:16.490
Or remove the cell.

7
00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:18.763
First, the readouts.

8
00:00:19.603 --> 00:00:23.103
For the clock, keep a mouse on a light-dark cycle and watch its running.

9
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:28.607
A mouse entrains if its running keeps a fixed timing relative to the cycle.

10
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:32.117
A mouse whose clock is not entrained free-runs.

11
00:00:32.837 --> 00:00:34.677
It drifts on its own internal period.

12
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.020
Separately, a brief light pulse at night tests how far the rhythm phase-shifts.

13
00:00:40.983 --> 00:00:42.723
Masking is a third readout.

14
00:00:43.583 --> 00:00:48.043
Bright light at night often suppresses a mouse’s running right away, independently

15
00:00:48.043 --> 00:00:49.203
of any clock shift.

16
00:00:50.123 --> 00:00:51.923
The same light can also shift the clock.

17
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:57.147
For the pupil, film the constriction over seconds to minutes of steady light.

18
00:00:58.117 --> 00:00:59.597
Row one is wild type.

19
00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:04.257
It entrains, shifts fully, and its pupil constricts fast and holds.

20
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.280
Row two has no melanopsin.

21
00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.600
It still entrains, but its phase shifts to brief pulses are smaller.

22
00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:16.973
Its pupil constricts, but in steady bright light the constriction fades over

23
00:01:16.973 --> 00:01:17.973
minutes.

24
00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:20.723
Row three has no rod or cone signalling.

25
00:01:21.663 --> 00:01:26.103
It entrains. Its pupil is slower and less sensitive, but it holds.

26
00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:29.887
In bright light, it reaches its normal maximum.

27
00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:32.767
Row four is the triple mutant.

28
00:01:33.627 --> 00:01:37.527
It lacks melanopsin, rod transducin and a cone channel subunit.

29
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:39.770
It does not entrain.

30
00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:44.870
It free-runs, shows no masking and has no significant pupil reflex.

31
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:47.273
Read down the table.

32
00:01:48.253 --> 00:01:51.593
Either input alone is enough to entrain and to constrict.

33
00:01:52.173 --> 00:01:53.713
Lose both, and nothing is left.

34
00:01:54.717 --> 00:02:00.057
Predict. Keep the rods and cones working, and ablate ipRGCs.

35
00:02:00.957 --> 00:02:03.677
If rods and cones had their own route, what would you see?

36
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:13.810
A strong separate route would let these mice keep much of their clock and pupil

37
00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:16.350
responses, like the melanopsin knockouts.

38
00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:19.470
Güler and colleagues found something else.

39
00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:24.710
The deficits were larger than with melanopsin loss alone, similar to those of the

40
00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:25.710
triple mutants.

41
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:30.023
Pattern vision, which runs through other ganglion cells, remained.

42
00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:34.683
So in mice, these cells are the main route to the clock and the pupil.

43
00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:40.633
An ablation removes both inputs, so it cannot say which input supplied what was

44
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:41.633
lost.

45
00:02:41.967 --> 00:02:45.007
Predict. Here are made-up phase shifts.

46
00:02:45.827 --> 00:02:48.567
Do the two single losses add up to the loss of both?

47
00:02:54.567 --> 00:03:00.047
They do not. Together the two single losses are smaller than the loss when both

48
00:03:00.047 --> 00:03:01.047
inputs go.

49
00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:04.887
One possible reading is that the output saturates.

50
00:03:05.627 --> 00:03:07.607
A pupil cannot get smaller than its minimum.

51
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.520
A phase shift has a ceiling too.

52
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.940
Near a ceiling, the stronger input alone gets close, and adding the other gains

53
00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:16.940
little.

54
00:03:17.233 --> 00:03:20.593
A single knockout shows what the other input can do alone.

55
00:03:21.473 --> 00:03:23.033
It does not show each input's share.

56
00:03:24.016 --> 00:03:30.296
One limit. In the Güler mice, a weakened toxin gene at the melanopsin locus kills

57
00:03:30.296 --> 00:03:34.096
the cells gradually and incompletely, and more are lost with age.

58
00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:38.650
Losing cells during development can let targets rewire.

59
00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.410
An ablation timed in adults is better.

60
00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.170
Remove the pigment, and the relay carries on.

61
00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:47.970
Remove the cell, and both inputs go.
